{"kind":"request","text":"S1: serving windows that one sender cannot fill\n\nThe attack-surface review of 10 November 2026 (planner request 79daff0f,\nat main 7177b87b) found that one sender fills the serving-window tables,\nand that the Worker then refuses every new caller. Read its sections 1.1\nto 1.4 and 1.6 before you start. The traces are in\n~/play/artroom-evidence/2026-11-10-attack-surface-review (trace.mjs and\ntrace-output.txt).\n\nOne head, with checker review. It closes five findings.\n\n1. Finding 1.1. `ServingLimits.account` makes no key window for a sender\n   whose address window is past its limit. Creation windows and\n   signed-read windows have separate caps. When a table is full, a new\n   identity is counted in one shared overflow window that has a limit.\n   The full table alone refuses nobody.\n2. Finding 1.2. The Site window keys an address with `addressKey`, so\n   the addresses of one IPv6 /64 are one key. The overflow rule of item\n   1 applies to the Site table.\n3. Finding 1.3. The sandbox service path passes the edge address to the\n   room Worker as an explicit argument of the service call. The Site\n   window and the serving windows use that address. Restrict the scope\n   IDs that the gateway admits to the installation's room if the cost is\n   small. If you do not, the note says why. Correct the comment in\n   apps/sandbox/worker.ts.\n4. Finding 1.4. The cheap decision comes before the expensive work. A\n   creation from an address past its limit is refused before the parse\n   of the body and before the signature check. You choose where the\n   check runs, and the note says so. If `has` answers from a cache, the\n   note says what bounds the cache and when a row leaves it.\n5. Finding 1.6. `found` on a register has a window for each key and a\n   window for each address.\n\nWitness. Traces 1 to 3 of trace.mjs become tests that fail at 7177b87b\nand pass at the head:\n- 8,191 requests from one address leave a new key at a new address able\n  to create and to read;\n- 4,096 addresses of one /64 leave a visitor at another address\n  admitted to the Site;\n- a visitor past the Site limit through the sandbox path does not close\n  Site reads for a visitor at another address.\nAdd one test for the `found` windows, and one test that a creation from\nan address past its limit costs no parse.\n\nDocuments. The head corrects the statements about these windows in\ndocs/security/entry-points.md (review, section 7: lines 156-157, 213,\n230, 279, 283 and 296 at 7177b87b).\n\nServing protection changes no native judgment and no authority (kernel\npage, section 7, R12). If the head adds a refusal or a reason to\n`contract`, the report says what it adds.\n\nOrder. The lane is open now. Its gate slot is after H. If the head\ncollides with identifiers (b) in packages/scope/src/worker.ts, say so\nand take the slot after identifiers (b). I deploy the Workers after the\nlanding.","body":{"conditions":"One head with checker review closes findings 1.1, 1.2, 1.3, 1.4 and 1.6 of review 79daff0f. Traces 1 to 3 of trace.mjs are tests that fail at 7177b87b and pass at the head. The head corrects docs/security/entry-points.md.","target_head":"7177b87be03ced9e2ddae2a5ebfcdfffcc1fda62","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}